   "post_data": {
    "post_id": 14122324,
    "post_type": "forum",
    "topic_id": 2013561,
    "poster_id": 363884,
    "post_rendered": "Let <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/1/d/51da37d984564162c87710ca27bea422f657fb73.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$O$\"  width=\"13\" height=\"12\" > be the circumcenter of the triangle <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/a/e2a559986ed5a0ffc5654bd367c29dfc92913c36.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$ABC$\"  width=\"42\" height=\"13\" >.</span> Points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/d/fed096cb12bb4627f07259bd946a58ff827aefa6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$D,E$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"37\" height=\"16\" > are on sides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/3/2/8327500afb9123f1b8bcfda5e3758342b848d8f1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC,AB$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"62\" height=\"16\" > and points <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/3/2/532a24574d3d201d320393c77a192198111629bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P,Q,R,S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"76\" height=\"16\" > are given in plane such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/e/c/aec8b3bf64eab3e47978459aed3b7367f070b7d7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$P,C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"34\" height=\"16\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/4/e/d4e1ed70ec950a5792330f281b4ec1a3ea4210d4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R,C$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"36\" height=\"16\" > are on different sides of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/7/e/57ee5125358c0606c9b588580ddfa66f83e607b7.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AB$\"  width=\"27\" height=\"13\" > and pints <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/a/e/9aeb1f3a5f1068988e8daa955107d54b23c8e142.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$Q,B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"36\" height=\"16\" > and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/c/3/3c3f114700540eec2f60fc4b708cd79dc3eb36b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$S,B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"33\" height=\"16\" > are on different sides of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/7/a179ff2638e4799cadd820db205c2beff6299ce9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AC$\"  width=\"27\" height=\"13\" > such that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/4/0e4e6e8815edd21accce939aafe23553cb26d9f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$R,S$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"34\" height=\"16\" > lie on circumcircle of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/c/a/4ca93ae729ddaccb62f6892fde53bb8071e4d54f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$DAP,EAQ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"91\" height=\"16\" > and <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/9/8/d985136e9ce2ac03e706507c2d917638becea1f2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\triangle BCE \\sim \\triangle ADQ , \\triangle CBD \\sim \\triangle AEP$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"295\" height=\"17\" >(</span>In that order), <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/2/9/8293a3dd161043618b4b42419442feb5c0fa4f38.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\angle ARE=\\angle ASD=\\angle BAC$\"  width=\"212\" height=\"13\" >,</span> If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/6/906074101ce543f1ad0f9b5a23926f22d487574c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$RS\\| PQ$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"63\" height=\"19\" > prove that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/6/9/16960223cb6a14147c96863fc14438677639801d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$RE ,DS$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"64\" height=\"16\" > are concurrent on <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/c/5/6c53cc93aa43cc55bea0dc2511833fe6bdda020f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$AO$\"  width=\"27\" height=\"13\" >.</span><br>\n<br>\n<i>Proposed by Alireza Dadgarnia</i>",
    "post_canonical": "Let $O$ be the circumcenter of the triangle $ABC$. Points $D,E$ are on sides $AC,AB$ and points $P,Q,R,S$ are given in plane such that $P,C$ and $R,C$ are on different sides of $AB$ and pints $Q,B$ and $S,B$ are on different sides of $AC$ such that $R,S$ lie on circumcircle of $DAP,EAQ$ and $\\triangle BCE \\sim \\triangle ADQ , \\triangle CBD \\sim \\triangle AEP$(In that order), $\\angle ARE=\\angle ASD=\\angle BAC$, If $RS\\| PQ$ prove that $RE ,DS$ are concurrent on $AO$.\n\n[i]Proposed by Alireza Dadgarnia[/i]",
    "username": "matinyousefi",
    "post_number": 1,
    "post_format": "bbcode",
    "category_name": "High School Olympiads",
    "category_id": 6,
    "poster_avatar": "//avatar.artofproblemsolving.com/avatar_363884.jpg?t=1662811789"
   }
