    "topic_id": 2014215,
    "poster_id": 363884,
    "post_rendered": "Given a function <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/2/da2928b21582c4072fa9de09b3bc9146181a9ee3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g:[0,1] \\to \\mathbb{R}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"101\" height=\"18\" > satisfying the property that for every non empty dissection of the trivial <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/6/e861e10e1c19918756b9c8b7717684593c63aeb8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$[0,1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"34\" height=\"18\" > to subsets <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/f/6/5f642cbbd390b4882363ab6b5c2906dbf6be6cc8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$A,B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"35\" height=\"16\" > we have either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/d/4/0d4626ac851def04341fe33ef96226d6ba9b8639.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\exists x \\in A; g(x) \\in B$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"134\" height=\"18\" > or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/5/e/45e9d817ceb2b6e289b43d1606d323c09e250cd9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\exists x \\in B; g(x) \\in A$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"135\" height=\"18\" > and we have furthermore <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/e/0/ce03f68623ed417d2a64d2980e88e0b715b1b6cf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g(x)&gt;x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"68\" height=\"18\" > for <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/4/d3423a3200d0ad7660fc4265d59d5598a53d551d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\in [0,1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" >.</span> Prove that there exist infinite <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/3/4/d3423a3200d0ad7660fc4265d59d5598a53d551d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x \\in [0,1]$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"67\" height=\"18\" > with <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/c/f5c5afbd0964cd82820fa474c08a5624ea80d086.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$g(x)=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"66\" height=\"18\" >.</span><br>\n<br>\n<i>Proposed by Ali Zamani </i>",
    "post_canonical": "Given a function $g:[0,1] \\to \\mathbb{R}$ satisfying the property that for every non empty dissection of the trivial $[0,1]$ to subsets $A,B$ we have either $\\exists x \\in A; g(x) \\in B$ or $\\exists x \\in B; g(x) \\in A$ and we have furthermore $g(x)>x$ for $x \\in [0,1]$. Prove that there exist infinite $x \\in [0,1]$ with $g(x)=1$.\n\n[i]Proposed by Ali Zamani [/i]",
