    "topic_id": 2014227,
    "poster_id": 363884,
    "post_rendered": "Given <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/2/29238f408d8cb5806f06cc03446dde0084ffe09d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"46\" height=\"15\" > prove that there exist infinite pairs of distinct natural numbers such that<br>\n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/2/1/f216f435991bb85b7ab784dc10646fb8a3a394fc.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\begin{align*}\nn+s(2n)=m+s(2m) \\\\\nkn+s(n^2)=km+s(m^2).\n\\end{align*}\"  width=\"205\" height=\"45\" ><span style=\"white-space:pre;\">(<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/3/2/f3270d4f89bb35e2aa73d733a13e0a84088abe30.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$s(n)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"32\" height=\"18\" ></span> denotes the sum of digits of <span style=\"white-space:pre;\"><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" >.</span>)<br>\n<br>\n<i>Proposed by Mohammadamin Sharifi</i>",
    "post_canonical": "Given $k \\in \\mathbb{Z}$ prove that there exist infinite pairs of distinct natural numbers such that\n\\begin{align*}\nn+s(2n)=m+s(2m) \\\\\nkn+s(n^2)=km+s(m^2).\n\\end{align*}\n($s(n)$ denotes the sum of digits of $n$.)\n\n[i]Proposed by Mohammadamin Sharifi[/i]",
