    "topic_id": 2014229,
    "poster_id": 363884,
    "post_rendered": "<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" > positive numbers are given. Is it always possible to find a convex polygon with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/7/0/a70670f99b13b346e700d7fec74ab5b49061d4af.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"41\" height=\"14\" > edges and a triangulation of it so that the length of the diameters used in the triangulation are the given <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/7/4/174fadd07fd54c9afe288e96558c92e0c1da733a.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n$\"  width=\"10\" height=\"8\" > numbers?<br>\n<br>\n<i>Proposed by Morteza Saghafian</i>",
